[{"id":7,"slug":"owney-the-dog-who-rode-the-mail-trains","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/owney-the-dog-who-rode-the-mail-trains/","date_gmt":"2026-10-25T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Owney, the dog who rode the mail trains"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Owney was a scruffy stray who became a regular at the post office in Albany, New York, in 1888. The National Postal Museum thinks his first owner was probably a postal clerk, a man the dog liked to walk to work. When that clerk moved away, Owney stayed behind with the friends he had made among the mail clerks.</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://ids.si.edu/ids/deliveryService?id=NPM-a.2008-41\" alt=\"Owney the dog, seated, in a studio portrait\"></p>\n<h2>A dog who followed the mailbags</h2>\n<p>The clerks let him spend the night in the building, and he slept on a heap of empty mailbags. Something about the bags held him, perhaps their texture, perhaps their scent. Where the bags went, Owney went. He rode with them on mail wagons first, then on mail trains, and before long he was traveling in Railway Post Office cars across New York State and then across the country.</p>\n<p>The railway mail clerks made him their unofficial mascot, and many came to think of him as a good luck charm. Train wrecks were all too common in those years, and the museum notes that no train Owney rode was ever in one.</p>\n<h2>Tags, tokens and a harness</h2>\n<p>People along his routes marked each visit by fastening something to his collar. The museum sorts the keepsakes that survive into groups, among them</p>\n<ul>\n<li>baggage checks and hotel room key tokens</li>\n<li>dog license tokens</li>\n<li>coins and medals</li>\n<li>tokens from fire departments, fraternal groups and political campaigns</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The collection grew heavy enough to weigh the dog down. Postmaster General John Wanamaker heard about it and had a harness made so the tags could be spread more evenly across Owney's body. Now and then a postal worker would take a few off and mail them to the Albany post office or to the Post Office Department in Washington, DC, and each time Owney came home to Albany the clerks there saved the tags. In 1894 a writer for the Brooklyn Daily Eagle, quoted by the museum, said the tags would \"jingle like the bells on a junk wagon.\"</p>\n<h2>Around the world, and after</h2>\n<p>In 1895 Owney went around the world, riding with mailbags on trains and steamships to Asia and across Europe before returning to Albany. The museum dates both of its photographs of him to about that time. The cover of this post is the second of them, with Owney in the doorway of a mail car and four unidentified clerks standing outside. Who took either picture is not known.</p>\n<p>In June 1897 he boarded a mail train for Toledo, Ohio. There a postal clerk showed him to a newspaper reporter, and Owney became ill tempered. The museum says the exact circumstances were never satisfactorily reported, but he died in Toledo of a bullet wound on June 11, 1897.</p>\n<p>The mail clerks raised money to have him preserved, and he was given to the Post Office Department's headquarters in Washington. In 1911 the department transferred him to the Smithsonian Institution, which has kept him ever since. He is on display in the National Postal Museum's atrium, wearing his harness, with several of his tags around him.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, records A.2008⁠-⁠41 and A.2006⁠-⁠47.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A stray dog wandered into the Albany post office in 1888 and became the unofficial mascot of the railway mail clerks."},"author":6,"categories":[4],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://ids.si.edu/ids/deliveryService?id=NPM-a.2006-47"}],"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":8,"slug":"mail-by-dog-sled-across-alaska","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/mail-by-dog-sled-across-alaska/","date_gmt":"2026-08-30T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Mail by dog sled across Alaska"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>A sepia postcard from about 1910 shows a group of unidentified mail contractors posing with their sled and dog team. The National Postal Museum keeps a photograph of the card. Its record says dog sleds carried mail in parts of the northern United States and the Alaskan Territory during the winter months, and that contract carriers used them across Alaska from the late nineteenth century into the early 1920s.</p>\n<h2>Why the mail went by dog team</h2>\n<p>Gold brought the crowds. In the 1890s thousands of Americans went north to the Klondike and Alaska, and word from home was one of the few comforts in a strange, hard country. It fell to the Post Office Department to keep those stampeders in touch with family thousands of miles away. When the rivers were free of ice, steamboats and canoes carried the mail. In the long dark winters most contractors used dog sleds, and remote settlements sometimes had no other contact with the outside world for much of the year.</p>\n<p>Letters were awaited with real hunger. At Valdez, mail piled up for several months because it could not be taken further north, and the postmaster, only recently appointed, panicked and fled both the post office and the territory. In Dawson, in Canada's Yukon Territory, Americans stood in hours long lines for their mail. In 1898, the museum notes, 90 percent of Dawson's population was American born.</p>\n<h2>Weight decided what traveled</h2>\n<p>Every pound counted for the dogs. Sled loads were not supposed to go over 400 pounds, though real loads varied a great deal. Carriers took first class letters and usually refused newspapers, magazines and packages unless there was room to spare. Those waited for spring, when a steamboat or wagon might bring them in. Some people slipped newspaper clippings and small gifts inside their letters, but a carrier who found an envelope suspiciously thick might leave it behind for the boats.</p>\n<p>The trails were dangerous. A letter written from Dawson in January 1902, now in the collections of the University of Alaska, Anchorage, reports that two mail carriers had drowned on unsafe ice and their mail was lost.</p>\n<h2>One family's sled</h2>\n<p>The museum owns a real mail sled. Contract carrier Ed Biederman, who lived from 1861 to 1945, used it on his 160 mile route between Circle and Eagle, Alaska. Sam Olson built it of hickory in 1922, with moose hide lashings, iron runners, a brake and springs, and cotton cords to hold the mail in place. Frostbite crippled Biederman in 1935 and he retired. His son Charlie took over the route, and his days as a dog sled mail carrier ended in 1938. Charlie kept the sled in the family and later gave it to his nephew, Max Beck, who donated it to the museum in 1995.</p>\n<p>Dog sleds stayed in use until airplanes became common in the territory. The museum dates the last regularly scheduled dog sled route in Alaska to 1963, when contract airplane service replaced Chester Noongwook's team on St. Lawrence Island. The Postal Service historian gives the same year for the last dogsled route, from Gambell to Savoonga.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2009⁠-⁠38.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Contract carriers used dog sleds across Alaska from the late nineteenth century into the early 1920s."},"author":6,"categories":[1],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":9,"slug":"the-first-woman-letter-carrier-in-chicago","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/the-first-woman-letter-carrier-in-chicago/","date_gmt":"2026-06-14T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Women letter carriers in Chicago"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The National Postal Museum has a photograph, dated 1944, of Jeanette Lee gathering mail from a street collection box. Its record calls her the first female letter carrier in Chicago, and the museum's research pages on women in the postal service caption her the same way. The record says nothing else about her, not her route and not how long she served. The photographer is unknown.</p>\n<h2>Women on city routes before 1944</h2>\n<p>Women carried mail in the United States long before 1944, mostly outside the cities. The Postal Service historian traces women on contract routes to about 1860 and on rural routes to 1899. City delivery, in the museum's words, stayed man's work well into the twentieth century.</p>\n<p>The first women known to deliver mail in an American city started on November 6, 1917, in Washington, DC. Permelia S. Campbell and Nellie M. McGrath took routes near Union Station as temporary substitute carriers. Their work was judged highly satisfactory, but after about two weeks they were taken off their routes and offered work indoors. Later that month the department asked eight of its largest post offices to test women as carriers for 15 days in December, when hundreds of extra carriers were hired for the Christmas mail anyway. That December, dozens of women delivered mail experimentally in Chicago, New York and St. Louis.</p>\n<p>So the museum's label needs a little care. Women walked Chicago routes in the 1917 test, as short term substitutes.</p>\n<h2>Two wars, two waves</h2>\n<p>After the First World War, most women carriers gave up their jobs to returning veterans. An order of 1902 had also kept women who married from being reappointed, and it stayed in force until November 1921, when Postmaster General Will Hays lifted the ban.</p>\n<p>The Second World War brought women back. In 1943 Postmaster General Frank Walker said the service would have to rely on many women and on men above draft age, though at first he said women would not carry the mail. The museum says he had to take that back once more than five percent of his carrier force was serving abroad. During the war women again worked as letter carriers in cities including New York, Chicago and Los Angeles. Lee's photograph comes from those years.</p>\n<p>After the war, most of these women left the service or were let go. In 1955 there were 95 women city carriers and 332 women rural carriers in the whole country, together less than one percent of all carriers. Change came in the 1960s. President John F. Kennedy ordered in 1962 that federal appointments and promotions be made without regard to sex, and the number of women city carriers rose from 104 in 1960 to 3,500 in 1968. By 2007 about 59,700 women served as city carriers.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠27.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Jeanette Lee gathers mail from a street collection box in a photograph dated 1944."},"author":6,"categories":[3],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://ids.si.edu/ids/deliveryService?id=NPM-a.2006-27"}],"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":10,"slug":"a-post-office-on-a-boston-streetcar","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/a-post-office-on-a-boston-streetcar/","date_gmt":"2026-04-19T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A post office on a Boston streetcar"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Railway Post Office streetcar number 642 was part of the Boston Elevated Railway. The photograph is dated about 1900.</p>\n<p>According to the record, the car traveled on Malden, Broadway, and Ferry streets between Charlestown and Post Office Square. The photographer is unknown.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠63.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Railway Post Office streetcar 642 ran between Charlestown and Post Office Square."},"author":6,"categories":[4],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":11,"slug":"delivering-the-mail-by-bicycle","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/delivering-the-mail-by-bicycle/","date_gmt":"2026-02-22T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Delivering the mail by bicycle"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>An unidentified city letter carrier paused along his daily rounds for this photograph from about 1890. The museum notes that some carriers chose bicycles to help speed their travel time.</p>\n<p>That is all the record says.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠33.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Some carriers chose bicycles to help speed their travel time."},"author":6,"categories":[3],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":12,"slug":"lieutenant-webb-and-the-first-day-of-airmail","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/lieutenant-webb-and-the-first-day-of-airmail/","date_gmt":"2026-01-11T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Lieutenant Webb and the first day of airmail"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>On May 15, 1918, the United States began scheduled airmail service between New York and Washington, DC, by way of Philadelphia. The Post Office Department ran it, but the planes and pilots came from the Army. A photograph from that day shows New York City Postmaster Thomas Patten handing Lieutenant Torrey Webb equipment as he makes ready for one of the first flights.</p>\n<h2>Why the Army flew the first mail</h2>\n<p>Postal officials had wanted an airmail service for years. Mail had been flown experimentally at air meets and fairs since 1911, and in 1917 Congress set aside $100,000 for experimental airmail in the next fiscal year. The department advertised for airplanes in February 1918 but called off the request a few weeks later. The Army Signal Corps wanted to run the service to give its pilots more cross country flying, so the Postmaster General and the Secretary of War agreed that the Army would lend its planes and pilots.</p>\n<p>Army Major Reuben H. Fleet was charged with setting it up. For railroads and steamboats the department had hired the owners of the lines, but there were no commercial airlines to hire. Fleet chose four of the pilots for the first day, Lieutenants Howard Culver, Torrey Webb, Walter Miller and Stephen Bonsal. Postal officials chose two more, Lieutenants James Edgerton and George Boyle.</p>\n<h2>Belmont Park to Bustleton</h2>\n<p>The service opened with takeoffs at the same time from the Polo Grounds in Washington and from Belmont Park on Long Island. The Army had set up a temporary airmail field at Belmont Park, and Webb took off from there with the first day's mail in a Curtiss Jenny numbered 38278. His leg ended at Bustleton Field, near Philadelphia, the midway stop of the route.</p>\n<p>The day did not go well everywhere. In Washington, Boyle climbed into his plane and could not start it, because it had not been fueled. Once in the air he got lost, ran low on gas and crash landed in rural Maryland, less than 25 miles from the city. The other flights that day ran as scheduled.</p>\n<p>The planes were simple. For the first three months the department flew six Army Curtiss JN-4H Jenny training planes. They had no reliable instruments or radios, and pilots found their way by landmarks and dead reckoning. Forced landings were frequent, but deaths in the early months were rare, largely because the planes were small, easy to handle and slow to land.</p>\n<h2>Webb's record</h2>\n<p>Webb graduated from Columbia and served in the Army before finishing his flight training at Ellington Field, where his good record brought him to Fleet's attention. As an airmail pilot he flew more than 3,500 miles and spent 45 hours in the air, with only one forced landing. On one flight he crashed his Jenny nose first into the ground and came out of it unhurt.</p>\n<p>The Army's part did not last. On August 12, 1918, the Post Office Department took over every part of the service, with civilian pilots and mechanics of its own and six mail planes built by the Standard Aircraft Corporation. Postage was 24 cents an ounce at first, including special delivery, and the public was slow to pay that for a trip only a few hours faster than the train. The rate fell to 16 cents on July 15, 1918, and to 6 cents on December 15.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2008⁠-⁠15.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"On May 15, 1918, the United States officially established airmail service between New York, Philadelphia and Washington, DC."},"author":6,"categories":[2],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":13,"slug":"sorting-mail-on-the-first-highway-post-office-bus","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/sorting-mail-on-the-first-highway-post-office-bus/","date_gmt":"2025-11-16T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Sorting mail on the first Highway Post Office bus"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Two Highway Post Office clerks sort mail inside the first Highway Post Office bus, in a National Postal Museum photograph dated 1941.</p>\n<p>The bus traveled between Washington, DC, and Harrisonburg, Virginia. A second photograph in the collection shows the bus in Harrisonburg and calls it the first trip of a Highway Post Office bus in 1941.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, records A.2006⁠-⁠77 and A.2006⁠-⁠79.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Two clerks sort mail inside the bus that ran between Washington, DC, and Harrisonburg, Virginia."},"author":6,"categories":[1],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://ids.si.edu/ids/deliveryService?id=NPM-a.2006-77"}],"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":14,"slug":"president-roosevelt-mails-a-letter-on-a-bus","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/president-roosevelt-mails-a-letter-on-a-bus/","date_gmt":"2025-08-24T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"President Roosevelt mails a letter on a bus"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>President Franklin D. Roosevelt is shown depositing a letter into the mail slot of the first Highway Post Office bus. The photograph is dated 1941.</p>\n<p>It is the same bus as the one in the photograph of the clerks sorting mail. The photographer is unknown.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠78.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Franklin D. Roosevelt deposits a letter into the mail slot of the first Highway Post Office bus in 1941."},"author":6,"categories":[1],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":15,"slug":"airmail-plane-38262-takes-off-on-the-first-day","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/airmail-plane-38262-takes-off-on-the-first-day/","date_gmt":"2025-05-04T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Airmail plane 38262 takes off on the first day"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Curtiss JN-4H &quot;Jenny&quot; airmail plane number 38262 is taking off from Washington, DC, on May 15, 1918, the first day of scheduled airmail service.</p>\n<p>The photograph is in the National Postal Museum Curatorial Photographic Collection. The photographer is unknown.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠14.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A Curtiss Jenny leaves Washington, DC, on May 15, 1918, the first day of scheduled airmail service."},"author":6,"categories":[2],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://ids.si.edu/ids/deliveryService?id=NPM-a.2006-14"}],"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":16,"slug":"inside-a-boston-mail-streetcar","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/inside-a-boston-mail-streetcar/","date_gmt":"2025-02-02T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Inside a Boston mail streetcar"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>This photograph from about 1899 shows the interior of a mail streetcar in Boston, Massachusetts. The record notes that the car was equipped with a canceling machine.</p>\n<p>Another Boston mail streetcar appears in a later post.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠54.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The interior of a Boston mail streetcar equipped with a canceling machine, about 1899."},"author":6,"categories":[4],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":17,"slug":"railway-post-office-car-number-488","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/railway-post-office-car-number-488/","date_gmt":"2024-10-13T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Railway Post Office car number 488"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The museum describes this car as a Pullman-built Railway Post Office car. Car number 488 was a baggage and Railway Post Office car from the Central of Georgia line.</p>\n<p>The photograph is dated about 1916 and the photographer is unknown.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠62.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A Pullman-built baggage and Railway Post Office car from the Central of Georgia line, about 1916."},"author":6,"categories":[4],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":18,"slug":"sorting-mail-in-the-tight-quarters-of-an-rpo-car","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/sorting-mail-in-the-tight-quarters-of-an-rpo-car/","date_gmt":"2024-07-21T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Sorting mail in the tight quarters of an RPO car"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Railway Post Office (RPO) clerks are at work sorting mail inside the tight quarters of an RPO car in this photograph from about 1920.</p>\n<p>The record gives no names and no place. The photographer is unknown.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠48.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Railway Post Office clerks at work inside a Railway Post Office car, about 1920."},"author":6,"categories":[4],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://ids.si.edu/ids/deliveryService?id=NPM-a.2006-48"}],"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":19,"slug":"the-deadwood-coach","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/the-deadwood-coach/","date_gmt":"2024-04-07T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Deadwood coach"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The National Postal Museum's record for this photograph is one sentence long. It shows a stagecoach on its way to Deadwood City, South Dakota, in 1889. The photographer is unknown, and no driver, passenger or company is named.</p>\n<h2>Why a coach carried the mail</h2>\n<p>Stagecoaches carried mail under contract from the earliest days of the postal system. In 1773 post road surveyor Hugh Finlay noted a stage driver who held a contract for semiweekly mail between Portsmouth, New Hampshire, and Boston, Massachusetts. In 1785 the Continental Congress told the postmaster general to award mail contracts to stagecoach operators, using postal money to help public travel and trade. Coaches were preferred over riders on horseback even when they cost more and were sometimes slower.</p>\n<p>An act of Congress in 1845 ended that preference. Contracts would now go to the lowest bidder promising \"celerity, certainty and security.\" Postal clerks shortened the phrase to three stars, and these became known as star routes. Even so, the department kept favoring coaches on some routes through the 1850s.</p>\n<p>For the stage companies, the mail was the money. The museum says mail contracts made up the bulk of their profits, so the company that won a route contract was the one most likely to succeed. The routes used by mail stages became lifelines into the territories, and settlers and fortune seekers soon traveled them.</p>\n<h2>Inside a mail coach</h2>\n<p>Concord style mail coaches were used from the early 1800s through the early 1900s. They took their name from Concord, New Hampshire, where Lewis S. Downing and J. Stephen Abbott designed and built them. They were painted in bright colors, with oil paintings on the doors, but built for rough roads. They came in six, nine and twelve passenger sizes, not counting riders on the roof when the inside was full. The mail usually rode in a space under the driver's seat, and baggage went on the roof rack or in the rear compartment.</p>\n<p>Room was always short. Up to eight people could be packed inside, with more on top and mailbags stuffed in among the passengers. Because a stage line was paid for its mail contract either way, drivers sometimes left mailbags by the side of the road to make room for a paying passenger's baggage, and the next stage occasionally picked them up. Coaches traveled day and night, stopping only to change horses at stations built every ten to fifteen miles.</p>\n<h2>A coach on the road</h2>\n<p>Star route contractors became known for moving the mail by whatever means worked, from boats to snowshoes, but most traveled by horse or horse drawn vehicle until the early twentieth century. For the towns along a route, the arrival of a coach carrying mail and passengers meant the latest news was at hand. The museum's own stagecoach exhibit uses a Library of Congress photograph from the John C. H. Grabill Collection titled <em>The Deadwood Coach</em>.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠83.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A stagecoach on its way to Deadwood City, South Dakota, in 1889."},"author":6,"categories":[1],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":20,"slug":"loading-the-mail-for-a-pathfinding-flight","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/loading-the-mail-for-a-pathfinding-flight/","date_gmt":"2024-01-14T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Loading the mail for a pathfinding flight"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>On July 29, 1920, General Superintendent of the Airmail Service L.B. Lent helped load mail into a Junkers Larson (JL-6) aircraft. It was one of three flown on a transcontinental pathfinding flight for the US Post Office Department.</p>\n<p>The plane, piloted by Harold &quot;Slim&quot; Lewis, carried some mail, as well as Lent, World War I aviation ace Eddie Rickenbacker, and Lieutenant Colonel H.H. Hartney of the US Air Service. The second and third Junkers aircraft were piloted by Mons Emil and Bert Acosta.</p>\n<p>The flights helped officials determine the best route for the upcoming transcontinental airmail route between New York, New York, and San Francisco, California.</p>\n<p>The record also quotes fellow airmail pilot Dean Smith on Lewis, who was one of the department&#39;s most colorful pilots. Smith remembered him as &quot;someone who could drink more and fly better than anyone else I knew.&quot; Lewis joined Boeing Air Transport after leaving the Post Office in 1927.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2009⁠-⁠26.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Three Junkers aircraft flew a transcontinental pathfinding flight for the Post Office Department in July 1920."},"author":6,"categories":[2],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":21,"slug":"a-letter-carrier-and-his-two-wheeled-mail-wagon","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/a-letter-carrier-and-his-two-wheeled-mail-wagon/","date_gmt":"2023-09-17T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A letter carrier and his two-wheeled mail wagon"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>This photograph from about 1895 shows an unidentified letter carrier with a horse-drawn, two-wheeled mail wagon.</p>\n<p>No location was given for the photograph. The record says the carrier&#39;s uniform signals a city delivery service.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2008⁠-⁠24.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"An unidentified carrier with a horse-drawn mail wagon, about 1895."},"author":6,"categories":[3],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://ids.si.edu/ids/deliveryService?id=NPM-a.2008-24"}],"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":22,"slug":"letter-carriers-leaving-new-yorks-main-post-office","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/letter-carriers-leaving-new-yorks-main-post-office/","date_gmt":"2023-05-28T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Letter carriers leaving New York's main post office"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>A group of letter carriers is photographed walking down the steps of New York City&#39;s main city post office at 33rd street. The photograph is dated about 1940.</p>\n<p>The photographer is unknown.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠26.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A group of carriers walk down the steps of the main city post office at 33rd street, about 1940."},"author":6,"categories":[3],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://ids.si.edu/ids/deliveryService?id=NPM-a.2006-26"}],"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":23,"slug":"a-row-of-one-man-mail-collection-wagons","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/a-row-of-one-man-mail-collection-wagons/","date_gmt":"2023-02-12T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A row of one-man mail collection wagons"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>In an unidentified city, postal officials pose with letter carriers and a row of one-man mail collection wagons. The photograph is dated about 1904.</p>\n<p>Carriers used the horse-drawn wagons to collect mail from city mailboxes. The screen wagons were painted blue with red and white features and gold leaf lettering, which a black and white photograph cannot show.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠70.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Postal officials pose with letter carriers and their horse-drawn collection wagons, about 1904."},"author":6,"categories":[3],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":24,"slug":"when-children-went-by-parcel-post","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/when-children-went-by-parcel-post/","date_gmt":"2022-11-20T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"When children went by parcel post"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>A city letter carrier posed for this humorous photograph around 1900 with a small boy riding in his mailbag. When it was taken, the Post Office Department was barred by law from carrying parcels over four pounds. Large packages went by private express companies, which had flourished since the mid 1800s.</p>\n<h2>Parcel post arrives</h2>\n<p>Rural Americans pushed for a change. Rural free delivery had brought mail to farm families, and they wanted food, dry goods, medicine and other goods that were hard to get in the country delivered too. Express companies and country merchants fought the idea for years. In 1910 rural residents made up 54 percent of the population, and they were just as determined. Congress authorized parcel post in an act of August 24, 1912, with an 11 pound limit. The service began on January 1, 1913, and 300 million parcels were mailed in its first six months.</p>\n<h2>The children who went by mail</h2>\n<p>A few weeks after the service started, a couple near Batavia, Ohio, sent their baby boy to his grandmother a short distance away. He weighed just under the 11 pound limit. A rural carrier picked him up and took him to her house in his mail wagon. The stamps cost 15 cents, and the parents insured him for $50. The museum and the Postal Service historian spell the family's and the carrier's names differently, so they are left out here.</p>\n<p>On January 27, 1913, a couple in Pine Hollow, Pennsylvania, handed their daughter to rural carrier James Byerly of Sharpsville, who delivered her safely to relatives in Clay Hollow that afternoon. Sending her cost 45 cents.</p>\n<p>The best known case came on February 19, 1914. May Pierstorff, just short of her sixth birthday, went from her parents' home in Grangeville, Idaho, to her grandparents' house in Lewiston, about 73 miles away, on 53 cents worth of stamps. She rode in the mail car with a relative who worked on the railway mail trains. As the museum points out, these children were not stuffed into sacks. They traveled in the care of postal workers their families trusted.</p>\n<h2>The rule, and the children after it</h2>\n<p>May's trip, along with a letter that month from a postmaster in Stratford, Oklahoma, who had noticed that no rule forbade mailing a person, led Postmaster General Albert Burleson to tell the nation's postmasters that human beings were barred from the mails. The photograph's record says the regulation came quickly once officials heard of such cases.</p>\n<p>It did not stop everyone. About a month later, a Maryland rural carrier took a 14 pound baby twelve miles from its grandmother's home in Clear Spring to its mother's house in Indian Springs, and a local paper reported that the baby slept the whole way. In 1915 six year old Edna Neff made the longest such trip, by railway mail train from her mother's home in Pensacola, Florida, to her father's in Virginia. That September postal officials investigated the trip of three year old Maud Smith in Kentucky, and a Railway Mail Service superintendent asked the local postmaster to explain why he had let her onto the train as parcel post. The museum's historian believed hers was the last case of child mail.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠22.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"After parcel post began in 1913, at least two children were sent through the mail."},"author":6,"categories":[3],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":25,"slug":"mail-arrives-in-omaha-at-1-50-in-the-morning","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/mail-arrives-in-omaha-at-1-50-in-the-morning/","date_gmt":"2022-07-10T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Mail arrives in Omaha at 1:50 in the morning"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Postal employees unload mail from the first regularly scheduled transcontinental airmail service. The airplane was the first to arrive flying from west to east. It reached Omaha, Nebraska, at 1:50 am on July 2, 1924.</p>\n<p>Pilots flew day and night, using beacons and field flood lights for navigation in the dark.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠8.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The first scheduled transcontinental airmail flight from west to east reached Omaha on July 2, 1924."},"author":6,"categories":[2],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}},{"id":26,"slug":"a-rural-carrier-in-harbor-springs-fitted-his-model-t-with-skis","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/back-issue/a-rural-carrier-in-harbor-springs-fitted-his-model-t-with-skis/","date_gmt":"2022-03-06T10:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A rural carrier in Harbor Springs fitted his Model T with skis"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>According to the caption on the back of this 1931 photograph, rural carrier Roy Aler of Harbor Springs, Michigan, stands next to his 1926 Model T.</p>\n<p>The car is fitted with a steel track on the rear drive shaft. That let him drop either wheels or skis into place in front, depending on weather conditions.</p>\n<p><em>From the National Postal Museum collection, record A.2006⁠-⁠82.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Roy Aler of Harbor Springs, Michigan, could drop either wheels or skis into place in front."},"author":6,"categories":[1],"tags":[5],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":6,"name":"Sorting Room","slug":"sortingroom"}]}}]